-- title:
--   Crossing-model constant π forces varpi to generate mathfrak m_{A∩ K}
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (the chosen algebraic closure of $\mathbb Q$), a nonzero level $N$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$; fix modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ vanishing on the pair of $q$-expansions), a proof `hKr` that its bivariate reduction mod $q$ equals $(X^q - Y)(X - Y^q)$, integrality hypotheses $h\alpha, h\beta$ for the Hecke $\bar\alpha$- and $\bar\beta$-maps at level $N$ and prime $q$, a place specialisation $P$ for these data, and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$. Let $K$ be a finite extension of $\mathbb Q$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $w$ a place of the modular function field `modularFunctionFieldC k N` over $k$, and $\varpi$ an element of the coefficient ring $A \cap K$ (the intersection of $A$ with $K$ as subrings of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$), which is assumed to be a discrete valuation ring; the node ring $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\,K\,w$ — the subring of functions in $R$'s node integers at $w$ whose Laurent expansions lie in the $K$-rational subfield at level $Nq$ — is assumed local. Let $W$ be a complete discrete valuation domain, complete for its maximal-ideal-adic topology, $\pi \in W$ irreducible, $E \ge 1$, and let $\iota$ be a ring isomorphism from the adic completion of $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\,K\,w$ at its maximal ideal onto the crossing model $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\,W\,(\pi^E)$, the quotient of $W[[U,V]]$ (formal power series in two variables) by the ideal `uvCrossingIdeal W (π^E)`. Assume $\iota$ carries the image in the completion of the constant function with value $\varpi$, namely $R.\mathrm{nodeConst}\,K\,w\,\varpi$, to $\mathrm{const}\,(\pi^E)\,\pi$, the class of the constant series $\pi$. Then the maximal ideal of $A \cap K$ is the ideal generated by $\varpi$.
--
--   The statement says that a constant in the coefficient ring $A \cap K$ which becomes the constant $\pi$ in a crossing presentation $W[[U,V]]/(UV-\pi^E)$ of the completed local ring at a node must itself be a uniformiser of $A \cap K$. It supplies the uniformiser hypothesis used in the subsequent analysis of orders of functions and of the two branches through a node in the reduction of $X_0(Nq)$.
